  "history": "Friend started as a verb form. In Old English, frēogan meant ‘to love, to care for’, and frēond was ‘a loving one’, like the -ing form loving. Its opposite was built in the same way: fēogan meant ‘to hate’, and fēond, ‘a hating one’, was an enemy. Later that word became fiend, an evil spirit or a cruel person. The same root for love and care is in free, which first meant ‘dear, belonging to the family’ (as opposed to a slave), and probably in Friday, the day of the goddess Frige. German Freund and Dutch vriend are close relatives. German Feind ‘enemy’ is the cousin of fiend.",
  "historyEinfach": "Friend is an old English word. It meant ‘a person who loves’. The old verb meant ‘to love’. There was also an opposite word: ‘a person who hates’. Today this word is fiend, a very bad person. In German, a friend is a Freund. In Dutch, it is a vriend.",
